type":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**How to read the thresholds**\n\n- The **$0.01-per-share and 0.5%-of-NAV** tests are **industry conventions, not SEC rules**. They trace to 1990s SEC staff positions and a never-adopted 1997 proposal.\n- Many policies treat an error as material only when it meets **both** tests. Below that, it is usually just corrected going forward. Each fund's written policy controls, and some are stricter or cumulative.\n\n**What governs correction**\n\n- The **board-approved NAV error policy** under the fund's compliance program (Rule 38a-1), together with valuation oversight under Rule 2a-5.\n- The **administration agreement**, which sets standards of care, indemnification, and who pays.\n- Prospectus disclosure and fiduciary and anti-fraud obligations, with escalation to the CCO and board.\n\n**ETF-specific nuance**\n\n- Secondary-market investors trade at market prices, not NAV. Economic harm therefore falls mainly on **authorized participants' creations and redemptions** and on the fund itself.\n- Remediation means reprocessing AP transactions or reimbursing the fund.\n- Under Rule 6c-11, the administrator should also correct published NAV, premium/discount and holdings data.","ttft_ms":4739,"type":"result","duration_ms":8849,"uuid":"79006fca-b308-4ba9-bc07-e55dc4fe6218","ttft_stream_ms":507,"time_to_request_ms":59,"first_content_frame_ms":508,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
